  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why do meerkats stand on guard?",
      "a": "Foraging meerkats dig with their heads down and cannot watch for predators. A sentinel standing up high spots eagles and jackals early, so the rest of the group can feed in safety. Group members take turns on duty.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a meerkat sentinel?",
        "Why do meerkats stand up on their back legs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do meerkats have different alarm calls?",
      "a": "Yes. Meerkats give different alarm calls for predators in the air and on the ground, and the calls also change with how urgent the danger is. Listeners respond differently, diving into burrows or looking up and scanning.",
      "asks": [
        "How do meerkats warn each other?",
        "Can meerkats tell an eagle from a jackal?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is being a sentinel dangerous?",
      "a": "Less than you might think. Sentinels usually go on duty after they have eaten well, and they often stand close to a burrow, so they are among the first to escape when danger comes.",
      "asks": [
        "Do meerkat guards get eaten?",
        "Why would a meerkat volunteer to guard?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do meerkats teach their pups?",
      "a": "Older helpers bring pups scorpions in stages: dead ones first, then live ones with the sting bitten off, and finally whole live ones, as the pups grow more skilled. It is one of the clearest examples of teaching in wild animals.",
      "asks": [
        "Do animals teach their young?",
        "How do meerkat pups learn to eat scorpions?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who leads a meerkat group?",
      "a": "A dominant female and male produce most of the pups. The other members, often their grown-up young, help by guarding, babysitting, digging burrows and feeding the pups.",
      "asks": [
        "Who is the boss of a meerkat mob?",
        "How are meerkat groups organised?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Meerkat alarm calls differ for aerial and ground predators and for how urgent the threat is (Manser, 2001).",
    "Adult meerkats teach pups to handle scorpions step by step (Thornton and McAuliffe, 2006)."
  ],
